5417 true] [17592186419633 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=-21-(-12)=-9.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $3∣(-9)$ because $(-9)÷3=-3,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=3.$\n\n- Note that $9∣(-9)$ because $(-9)÷9=-1,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=9.$\n\n- Note that $12∤(-9).$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=12.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534339 17592186045417 true] [17592186419634 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419628 13194139534339 17592186045417 true] [17592186419634 1069 "q-76962" 13194139534339 17592186045417 true] [17592186419634 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534339 17592186045417 true] [17592186419634 1071 "Given that $19≡5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=7$\n- $n=9$\n- $n=2$" 13194139534339 17592186045417 true] [17592186419634 1072 #uuid "7dad68af-5609-5774-82ac-907f4e4304f5" 13194139534339 17592186045417 true] [17592186419634 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=19-5=14.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $7∣14$ because $14÷7=2,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=7.$\n\n- Note that $9∤14.$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=9.$\n\n- Note that $2∣14$ because $14÷2=7,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534339 17592186045417 true] [17592186419635 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419630 13194139534339 17592186045417 true] [17592186419635 1069 "q-103380" 13194139534339 17592186045417 true] [17592186419635 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534339 17592186045417 true] [17592186419635 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $-7≡27\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)$\n- $-5≡15\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$\n- $11≡18\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$" 13194139534339 17592186045417 true] [17592186419635 1072 #uuid "c37fbba7-25b2-5279-9899-188d8dedca8a" 13194139534339 17592186045417 true] [17592186419635 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false because $5∤(-7-27).$ Notice that $-7-27=-34,$ which is not divisible by $5.$\n\n- Statement II is false because $6∤(-5-15).$ Notice that $-5-15=-20,$ which is not divisible by $6.$\n\n- Statement III is true because $7\\,|\\,(11-18).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(11-18)÷7=-7÷7=-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534339 17592186045417 true] [17592186419636 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419629 13194139534339 17592186045417 true] [17592186419636 1069 "q-113664" 13194139534339 17592186045417 true] [17592186419636 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534339 17592186045417 true] [17592186419636 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $-5≡8\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$\n- $-23≡-5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$\n- $-7≡-1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)$" 13194139534339 17592186045417 true] [17592186419636 1072 #uuid "e9350880-d00e-5d41-adb3-52fc6c8e0846" 13194139534339 17592186045417 true] [17592186419636 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false because $7∤(-5-8).$ Notice that $-5-8=-13,$ which is not divisible by $7.$\n\n- Statement II is true because $6∣(-23-(-5)).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(-23-(-5))÷6=-18÷6=-3\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true because $2\\,|\\,(-7-(-1)).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(-7-(-1))÷2=-6÷2=-3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534339 17592186045417 true] [17592186419637 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419632 13194139534339 17592186045417 true] [17592186419637 1069 "q-113675" 13194139534339 17592186045417 true] [17592186419637 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534339 17592186045417 true] [17592186419637 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $-36≡-19\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$\n- $15≡-26\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)$\n- $-27≡9\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$" 13194139534339 17592186045417 true] [17592186419637 1072 #uuid "ee3460a5-a9ce-5793-8247-ccd3db242273" 13194139534339 17592186045417 true] [17592186419637 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false because $7∤(-36-(-19)).$ Notice that $-36-(-19)=-17,$ which is not divisible by $7.$\n\n- Statement II is false because $10∤(15-(-26)).$ Notice that $15-(-26)=41,$ which is not divisible by $10.$\n\n- Statement III is true because $4\\,|\\,(-27-9).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(-27-9)÷4=-36÷4=-9\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534339 17592186045417 true] [17592186419638 1058 #edb/ref 17592186419639 13194139534339 17592186045417 true] [17592186419638 1062 #uuid "50b54d0a-1dd6-5c1c-9143-afdb86424602" 13194139534339 17592186045417 true] [17592186419638 1063 #edb/ref 17592186419637 13194139534339 17592186045417 true] [17592186419639 1062 #uuid "b6185db5-f13a-5f65-96d8-0e2c10a7500a" 13194139534339 17592186045417 true] [17592186419639 1063 #edb/ref 17592186419633 13194139534339 17592186045417 true] [17592186419640 1058 #edb/ref 175921